Because it focuses on what has arisen, it is attachment; because it focuses on the <term>co-emergent joy</term> <gloss>sahajananda; the innate bliss realized in advanced tantric practice</gloss> that has just ceased, it is the absence of attachment. Because it is lower than those, the first joy itself is the <term>middle</term> <gloss>madhyama</gloss>. <note>The text is categorizing different stages of meditative bliss or joy.</note> The appearance is called the "intermediate one," just as one speaks of an "intermediate body" or an "intermediate feeling." Any of those three states does not become the finality. The phrase "the end of the supreme joy of the first distinction" refers to that which has just passed being made perfectly clear. 

The phrase "this is supreme great bliss" means this is the excellence of great bliss itself, which is the meaning of <term>co-emergent joy</term>. In order to explain the certainty of the moment through that very state, it is said: "There is no self, and there is no other." This means that in that very state, the appearance of another as the perceived and the appearance of oneself as the perceiver do not exist. It is perfectly clear through the infinity of non-duality and non-perception, which is merely the ultimate bliss. This is the meaning of <term>natural joy</term> <gloss>prakritiananda</gloss>.

As it is said in the <term>Chapter on Gathering</term>:
"Joy is a slight bliss;
Supreme joy increases beyond that;
Special joy becomes the absence of attachment;
The remaining is co-emergent joy.
The first is the desire for contact;
The second is the desire for bliss;
The third is the destruction of bliss;
Therefore, the fourth is what should be meditated upon.
Supreme joy is described as craving;
Nirvana is the absence of joy;
The middle is merely joy itself;
Co-emergent joy abandons all of these."

From the <term>Chapter on Empowering the Disciple</term>, it is said:
"There is no attachment and no absence of attachment;
Even in the middle, nothing is perceived;
Because the three are separated,
It is called the perfectly awakened nature."

And also:
"Either everything is the self,
Or everything is completely abandoned;
The first distinction to be made supreme
Is the abandonment of the three joys."

That which is to be meditated upon in that way is the fourth joy. Also from the <term>Chapter on Empowering the Disciple</term>:
"The first is merely joy;
Supreme joy is the second in number;
The third is explained as special joy;
The fourth is called the co-emergent."

This shows that special joy is the third. If one asks how the nature of the fourth is explained, after counting the three joys which are to be abandoned, then the co-emergent joy which is to be adopted is reached.

<summary>This page explains the four types of joy in tantric practice, defining co-emergent joy as the ultimate non-dual state that transcends attachment and the other three joys. It cites various chapters from the tantras to establish the hierarchy and meditative significance of these states of bliss.</summary>
